          "secNumber": "5.3",
          "heading": "Reductions",
          "content": "**Reductions: conceptual explanation**\n\n### Mapping reductions with directions intact\nA many-one reduction from problem A to problem B is a total computable function f with x\u2208A if and only if f(x)\u2208B. It transforms each A instance into a B instance while preserving yes/no membership. If B were decidable, we could decide A by computing f(x) and then calling the B decider. Thus, if A is undecidable and A\u2264_m B, B must also be undecidable. The arrow's direction matters. Reducing an easy problem to a difficult one proves no hardness claim; reducing an already impossible problem to the candidate target transfers impossibility.\n\nTo reduce halting to a semantic program property, take a machine M and input w and construct a new program P that first simulates M(w), then performs a specified action only if the simulation halts. Whether P ever performs that action corresponds to whether M halts. The constructed P can contain a fixed description of M and w, so producing it is a finite, effective compilation procedure even if running P is unbounded. The construction must handle both branches: if M halts, the target property holds; if M does not halt, the target property fails.\n\nDon't shortcut a reduction with a vague claim that 'both problems are hard.' Write the transformed program or instance, argue that the transformation always terminates, and establish both implications. A carefully crafted reduction often teaches more than a list of undecidable targets because it reveals exactly which seemingly useful analyzer would solve an impossible general problem.\n\n### Constructing reductions in program analysis\nSuppose an analyzer A decides whether arbitrary code can ever reach a line printing ALERT. To use A to decide the halting problem, construct for each pair (M,w) a wrapper program P_{M,w} whose first action is to simulate M on w. If the simulation terminates, P prints ALERT; otherwise it remains in the simulation forever. The transformation from encoded M,w to encoded P is finite and computable because it only embeds descriptions and a fixed wrapper. Consequently A(P) answers yes if and only if M halts on w.\n\nWrite both cases formally. If M halts on w after t steps, P completes the simulation after finite overhead and reaches the print statement, so A must say yes. If M never halts on w, control in P never reaches that statement, so A must say no. A then becomes a total halting decider, contradicting the theorem. The mapping is a reduction from HALT to REACH-ALERT. Reversing the names or proving only the first case would not suffice.\n\nThe same blueprint can transfer undecidability to other unrestricted behavioral properties, provided the constructed program makes the target property hinge exactly on the source computation. Avoid assuming that a property is undecidable merely because it sounds difficult. Syntax properties such as 'contains an if statement' are easy to decide by parsing. Termination questions for programs with statically bounded loops can be decidable, even if general termination is not.\n\nIn a lab, implement the wrapper for a deliberately limited interpreter and test it against known terminating and looping example programs. Such code demonstrates the mapping pattern, but the theorem for all Turing machines relies on an ideal model capable of arbitrary simulation. Label the bounded interpreter's conclusions honestly.",
