          "secNumber": "5.4",
          "heading": "Rice-Type Results",
          "content": "**Rice-Type Results: conceptual explanation**\n\n### Rice's theorem and semantic properties\nRice's theorem states that every nontrivial semantic property of the partial function computed by a Turing-machine program is undecidable. Nontrivial means that some computable partial functions have the property and others do not. Semantic means it depends on program behavior, not on syntax such as the presence of a particular variable name or how many source lines appear. A syntax checker can decide whether code contains the token `while`, but a general analyzer cannot decide whether arbitrary code computes the everywhere-zero function, provided the formalized property is nontrivial.\n\nA reduction proof of the Rice-style principle can embed a halting computation into a program whose observable function changes depending on whether the embedded computation terminates. The exact construction must account for the empty function and distinguish semantic behavior from structural text. This is why one cannot infer from Rice's theorem that all practical static analysis is useless. A tool can decide restricted properties, analyze restricted languages, produce sound approximations that sometimes answer 'unknown', or find bugs without proving their absence universally.\n\nFor example, a compiler may conservatively flag a possible division-by-zero risk even on a safe program because its analysis overapproximates reachable states. Conversely, a bounded symbolic executor can prove safety for a limited number of steps. Those are engineering compromises compatible with undecidability: the theorem rules out a total, always-correct decider for the unrestricted property, not every informative analysis.\n\n### What Rice's theorem actually rules out\nRice's theorem concerns properties of the partial function computed by a program. The word 'semantic' is not enough by itself; the property must be extensional, meaning programs computing the same partial function agree on whether the property holds. The predicate 'has exactly twelve instructions' is syntactic and not subject to Rice's conclusion because equivalent programs can have different lengths. The predicate 'returns zero on every input' is semantic; the predicate 'halts on at least one input' is also semantic under the usual partial-function model. Both are nontrivial because some computed functions satisfy them and others do not.\n\nA real linter can still flag an always-false branch in a decidable restricted subset. A model checker for a finite transition graph can determine reachability. A theorem prover can verify a loop invariant supplied by the user. None of this contradicts Rice's theorem: the theorem concerns a total decider for arbitrary Turing-complete programs and nontrivial semantic properties. Useful tools commonly sacrifice some combination of generality, precision, completeness, or guaranteed termination.\n\nConsider the three-value analysis result PASS, FAIL, UNKNOWN. If the analysis is sound for declaring PASS, PASS means it has proved the desired property under stated assumptions. FAIL may mean a concrete counterexample has been found or, in some tools, that proof failed; do not conflate those meanings. UNKNOWN explicitly records that the engine could not decide. If an analyzer never reports UNKNOWN yet advertises unrestricted correctness and termination, its marketing conflicts with fundamental undecidability.\n\nDesign a user interface that displays scope and assumptions alongside the result. A result about a finite bound of execution should not visually resemble an unbounded proof. Mathematical limitations become actionable design requirements when the tool honestly distinguishes certainty levels.",
          "simulations": [
            "toc-u5-b"
          ]
